WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:09.898
Install GNU Hello, the classic tiny test program, with a normal package manager, and it lands at slash usr, slash bin, slash hello.

00:00:10.148 --> 00:00:20.457
Install it with Nix, and it lands somewhere stranger: a directory in slash nix, slash store, whose name starts with thirty-two characters of what looks like noise.

00:00:20.707 --> 00:00:24.722
That noise is a hash, and every package Nix installs has one.

00:00:25.972 --> 00:00:31.941
Nix, and NixOS, the Linux distribution built on it, make some unusual promises.

00:00:31.991 --> 00:00:36.309
Build the same thing twice and, as far as possible, get the same result.

00:00:36.359 --> 00:00:39.045
Keep two versions of a package side by side.

00:00:39.095 --> 00:00:42.945
Roll back an upgrade, for one program or for the whole machine.

00:00:43.195 --> 00:00:45.104
Why is there a hash in every path?

00:00:45.154 --> 00:00:47.918
And how does that one idea deliver all of that?

00:00:51.668 --> 00:00:55.119
Most package managers put everything in shared places.

00:00:55.169 --> 00:00:57.863
Programs go in slash usr, slash bin.

00:00:57.913 --> 00:01:00.689
Libraries go in slash usr, slash lib.

00:01:00.939 --> 00:01:05.171
Upgrade a library, and the new version overwrites the old one in place.

00:01:05.221 --> 00:01:10.505
Every program that used the old one now gets the new one, whether it was built for it or not.

00:01:10.755 --> 00:01:16.512
Two programs that need different versions of the same library often can't both have what they need.

00:01:16.562 --> 00:01:22.151
Some libraries put a version number in the file name, but most things get exactly one path.

00:01:22.401 --> 00:01:30.879
And an upgrade that stops halfway, say the power goes out, leaves some files new and some old: a system that matches no version at all.

00:01:32.629 --> 00:01:34.613
Nix starts from a different picture.

00:01:34.663 --> 00:01:38.977
Every package is built from a recipe, which Nix calls a derivation.

00:01:39.027 --> 00:01:50.518
Nix treats that build as a pure function of its inputs, and the inputs are everything the build uses: the source code, the build script, the compiler, the libraries, and every option.

00:01:50.768 --> 00:01:56.517
On Linux, Nix runs each build isolated by default, so it can use only what it declared.

00:01:56.567 --> 00:02:01.054
Even a downloaded source file has to match a hash written in the recipe.

00:02:01.304 --> 00:02:09.619
Nix computes a hash over all of those inputs, and names the output after it: slash nix, slash store, the hash, then the package name.

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:12.157
It can do that before building anything.

00:02:12.208 --> 00:02:18.976
On this machine, Nix computed Hello's path from its recipe, and the package then landed exactly there.

00:02:20.026 --> 00:02:22.513
So the hash is not a checksum of what came out.

00:02:22.563 --> 00:02:24.743
It's a name for everything that went in.

00:02:26.693 --> 00:02:28.286
Now change one input.

00:02:28.336 --> 00:02:32.172
Here, one option of Hello's build: whether to run its tests.

00:02:32.422 --> 00:02:37.304
The recipe is different, so the hash is different, and the new Hello gets a new path.

00:02:37.554 --> 00:02:39.771
A small script called greet runs Hello.

00:02:39.821 --> 00:02:43.854
Its inputs include Hello's path, so greet gets a new path too.

00:02:43.904 --> 00:02:46.514
The change ripples to everything built from it.

00:02:46.764 --> 00:02:50.777
Cowsay doesn't use Hello, so its path stays exactly the same.

00:02:51.827 --> 00:02:54.365
And nothing that was already built gets touched.

00:02:54.415 --> 00:03:00.048
The old Hello and the new Hello now sit side by side in the store, each in its own directory.

00:03:00.298 --> 00:03:06.179
That's how two versions coexist: they can't overwrite each other, because they never share a path.

00:03:07.929 --> 00:03:09.866
But Hello isn't self-contained.

00:03:09.916 --> 00:03:12.992
It needs other things to run, like the C library.

00:03:13.042 --> 00:03:16.556
So where does it find them, if not in slash usr, slash lib?

00:03:16.806 --> 00:03:20.982
Its files refer to them by their full store paths, hashes included.

00:03:21.232 --> 00:03:25.872
Nix finds those references by scanning the build's output for store paths.

00:03:25.922 --> 00:03:32.494
Follow them, and the references of those, and you get the package's closure: everything it needs at run time.

00:03:32.744 --> 00:03:47.288
Hello's closure is five paths: Hello itself, the C library, a library the C library pulls in, another that one needs in turn, and a small runtime library that ships with the compiler and that compiled programs use.

00:03:48.538 --> 00:03:53.537
Copy those five paths to another machine of the same kind, and Hello runs there.

00:03:53.587 --> 00:03:58.331
And things needed only to build it, like the compiler itself, aren't in the list.

00:03:59.881 --> 00:04:02.531
So what does it mean to have a program installed?

00:04:02.781 --> 00:04:08.388
In Nix, what you have installed is a generation: a small directory of links into the store.

00:04:08.438 --> 00:04:11.079
Your profile is a link to one generation.

00:04:11.329 --> 00:04:13.974
Install Hello: that's generation one.

00:04:14.024 --> 00:04:22.272
Install cowsay: Nix creates generation two, with links to both, and then switches the profile's link to it, in a single step.

00:04:22.522 --> 00:04:25.760
There's no moment when the profile is half old and half new.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.624
It points to one generation, then to the other.

00:04:29.474 --> 00:04:32.778
Roll back, and the link switches back to generation one.

00:04:32.828 --> 00:04:38.078
Hello is there, cowsay is gone from the profile, and nothing in the store was deleted.

00:04:39.128 --> 00:04:41.909
Old generations stay until you delete them.

00:04:41.959 --> 00:04:47.305
Only then can Nix's garbage collector remove the store paths nothing else uses.

00:04:48.255 --> 00:04:52.871
NixOS applies the same idea to an entire operating system.

00:04:53.121 --> 00:05:01.868
You describe the system in configuration.nix, a file that can pull in others: the packages, the settings, the services to run.

00:05:01.918 --> 00:05:08.481
Nix builds all of it, the programs, the configuration files, the start-up scripts, into one store path.

00:05:08.731 --> 00:05:12.618
Here's a minimal configuration, evaluated on this machine.

00:05:12.668 --> 00:05:14.223
It has one system path.

00:05:14.273 --> 00:05:19.504
Add one line, turning on the nginx web server, and the system gets a different path.

00:05:19.554 --> 00:05:23.435
Remove the line, and the path is exactly the first one again.

00:05:24.685 --> 00:05:30.783
Switching to a new configuration makes a new system generation, and adds it to the boot menu.

00:05:30.833 --> 00:05:37.789
If an upgrade goes wrong, you pick the previous generation when the machine starts, or roll back from the command line.

00:05:38.039 --> 00:05:41.254
The link to the new generation switches in one step.

00:05:41.304 --> 00:05:48.841
Bringing the running machine in line with it takes longer: services restart one by one, and a new kernel needs a reboot.

00:05:50.391 --> 00:05:52.555
The hash promises the same inputs.

00:05:52.605 --> 00:05:54.882
It doesn't always promise the same bytes.

00:05:55.132 --> 00:06:01.497
Some builds stamp in the date, or vary in small ways from run to run: same path, different bytes.

00:06:01.547 --> 00:06:08.344
A 2025 study rebuilt about seven hundred thousand packages from old Nixpkgs snapshots.

00:06:08.394 --> 00:06:14.665
Between sixty-nine and ninety-one percent came out bit-for-bit identical, rising over the years.

00:06:15.715 --> 00:06:18.279
And the inputs are only fixed if you fix them.

00:06:18.329 --> 00:06:22.383
Nixpkgs, the collection the recipes come from, keeps moving.

00:06:22.433 --> 00:06:26.097
To get the same inputs next year, pin the exact revision.

00:06:26.347 --> 00:06:29.853
Rollback switches the software and its configuration.

00:06:29.903 --> 00:06:31.433
It doesn't touch your data.

00:06:31.483 --> 00:06:35.817
Say a new version of a database converted its files to a new format.

00:06:35.867 --> 00:06:43.205
Roll back, and you get the old program, but the files stay converted, and the old program may not be able to read them.

00:06:44.955 --> 00:06:46.635
So why a hash in every path?

00:06:46.685 --> 00:06:50.278
Because the path names a build by everything that went into it.

00:06:50.528 --> 00:06:52.996
That's what keeps anything from being overwritten.

00:06:53.046 --> 00:06:56.183
Versions coexist because they never share a path.

00:06:56.233 --> 00:07:00.269
Dependencies are exact, because they're referred to by their paths.

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:05.582
And an upgrade, or a rollback, is switching one link, for a program or for a whole system.

00:07:05.832 --> 00:07:07.984
What the hash fixes is the inputs.

00:07:08.034 --> 00:07:13.315
Pin them, and you get the same recipe again; the bytes usually, but not always, follow.

00:07:13.365 --> 00:07:16.075
And back up your data, because rollback won't.
